] [17592186250024 1111 #uuid "23c0a00e-4526-5472-865c-33dc5a708088" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250025 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250025 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533769 true] [17592186250025 1111 #uuid "7ca19fa0-327a-5758-a43f-13ca69c44585" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250026 1034 "II only" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533769 true] [17592186250026 1111 #uuid "abadd53d-36c3-5af6-99bb-1b102fa335d0" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250028 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1089 "q-80352" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941629/assets/1956e8c684cc03538877d3ec5d1e72d3d3f48b63341f7739b9a18962b080f1e3.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1113 #uuid "f48448f1-4b70-5bcf-83fd-82afeaf27fb8" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250027 1145 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=1,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=-x(x+2)^{3}(x-1)^{3}.$" 13194139533769 true] [17592186250028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250029 13194139533769 true] [17592186250028 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250030 13194139533769 true] [175